WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.960
One kinesin molecule rides a bead along a microtubule in a dish.

2
00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.220
It moves in jumps of 8 nanometres.

3
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.340
At low load, each jump costs 1 ATP.

4
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.480
These numbers come from purified proteins, in vitro.

5
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.140
But how do you watch one molecule step?

6
00:00:25.033 --> 00:00:28.513
A gliding assay shows many motors at once.

7
00:00:29.073 --> 00:00:31.473
To see one, use an optical trap.

8
00:00:32.213 --> 00:00:35.673
A focused laser holds a small bead near its centre.

9
00:00:36.473 --> 00:00:41.173
Pull the bead off centre, and the trap pulls it back, like a weight on a spring.

10
00:00:42.033 --> 00:00:46.773
The force is the trap stiffness, kappa, times the distance from the centre.

11
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:52.543
Here is a worked problem, with made-up data in the range of real measurements.

12
00:00:53.443 --> 00:00:58.123
At low load, the bead moves 400 nanometres in half a second.

13
00:00:58.723 --> 00:01:01.123
That is 800 nanometres per second.

14
00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:06.703
Divide by the 8-nanometre step, and you get 100 steps per second.

15
00:01:06.703 --> 00:01:11.723
Near zero load, kinesin uses 1 ATP per step.

16
00:01:12.343 --> 00:01:16.383
So this motor burns about 100 ATP every second.

17
00:01:17.317 --> 00:01:20.017
Now let the bead pull against the trap.

18
00:01:20.737 --> 00:01:25.297
The trap stiffness is 0.05 piconewtons per nanometre.

19
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:31.257
The motor walks until it stalls, with the bead 120 nanometres from the centre.

20
00:01:32.377 --> 00:01:34.357
Force is stiffness times distance.

21
00:01:35.137 --> 00:01:39.757
That is 6 piconewtons at the top of the range that was measured, 5 to 6.

22
00:01:40.550 --> 00:01:42.330
How much work is that?

23
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:46.150
Work per step is force times distance.

24
00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:52.530
6 piconewtons times 8 nanometres is 48 piconewton-nanometres.

25
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.950
Assume that one mole of ATP releases about 50 kJ.

26
00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.430
That is 83 piconewton-nanometres for each molecule.

27
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:08.670
The true value depends on the concentrations of ATP, ADP and phosphate.

28
00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:13.990
At stall, 48 over 83 is about 58 percent.

29
00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:21.420
Two cautions. The bead moves as far as the motor only if the link between them is

30
00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.700
stiff. A floppy tether stretches, and the bead under-reports the step.

31
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.440
And never combine numbers from different experiments.

32
00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:36.140
One muscle myosin moved its filament about 11 nanometres at low load.

33
00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.280
Held still, it pulled with 3 to 4 piconewtons.

34
00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.840
Those are two loading conditions.

35
00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.820
Keep every number tied to the preparation that measured it.
